why quality important to you and how did  you begin working from serie first I  should say I was a primary care doc  working in rural remote Northern Ontario  and seeing lots of patients and the  hospital in my clinic and really you  know losing a lot of sleep over how to  give better care in a really resource  constrained setting for Canada that was  a pretty difficult setting and it was a  tough it was a tough job and I think we  struggled on how to do that and I think  we did the best we could in those  circumstances which are still so much  better than what most of the world faces  of course today so I've been thinking  about quality to professional for a long  time  and then I think you know it came to  global health and Public Health and  trying to understand people and patients  and what are we seeing some population  surveys for example it struck me at the  very beginning of my research career  that people are using some services and  not others and so I started asking why  myiasis why are you skipping facilities  why are you bypassing why are you using  the facility for antenatal care but you  would never come back for delivery  what's going on with that yes and that's  actually when the first signs of the  importance of quality emerged from from  our research and the more work I did  with my colleagues in Africa the more  people we asked the more experiments we  performed and the more I read the more I  realized that quality is fundamental to  people in every part of the world and  for every health condition yes they  define it a bit differently you know  people who have a sick child maybe are  immediately focused on the competence of  the provider whereas women for delivery  are more focused on the interpersonal  care but they all want to get better  they all want strong skills and and  respect so it's that body of evidence  and work that really shifted my thinking  too hard to asking the question what's  actually going on in facilities that  people demanded their actions are based  on quality what's actually happening in  the health system and so for the last  several years our team have been looking  at just that question and that's also  leads to to this work on the Atlantic  Global Health Commission to try to  interrogate the the point of  intersection between systems  and users because that's where health  care happens and that's where health is  built it's at least in terms of the  Health Systems contribution to  population health that's where all the  work is done so understanding what  people expectations are and  understanding what it is they actually  get has been a huge preoccupation for me  I think if we don't attend to that the  health system is is I think we forgo a  massive opportunity to improve  population health because I also should  take its place as one of the  determinants of health yes
 

